notes               = "Untitled in source. Rhymed on -اد throughout (تابید / داد / شاد است / آزاد است) and it repeats its own opening four lines at the midpoint — a refrain structure, not a printing fault. بگوشم and نمیدانم are printed without the zero-width joiner in lines 1 and 3 and نمی‌دانم with it in line 13; kept exactly as printed. The first line is another sense-crossing: morning light arrives in the ear. Bare year ۱۳۸۲ → +621 = 2003. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

بگوشم نور صبح حنجرت تابید
و بیداری سبزی گوش‌هایم را نوازش داد
نمیدانم که بوی ابر می‌آید
و یا غم در دلم شاد است.
صدایی مهربان داری که با مهتاب همراه است
بخوان تا انتظار خاک تر گردد
سفر کردست بی‌مهتاب.
بخوان
آغوش واکرده که با مهتاب برگردد.
خیالت از سفر آمد
و روی سایه‌ها خوابید
بگوشم نور صبح حنجرت تابید.
نمی‌دانم که بوی ابر می‌آید
و یا غم در دلم شاد است.
صدایی مهربان داری
که با مهتاب همزاد است
صدا از ماه می‌آید
بخوان
آواز آزاد است.
تهران ۱۳۸۲

===machine===

The morning light of your throat shone into my ear
and a green wakefulness stroked my ears.
I cannot tell whether the smell of cloud is coming
or whether the grief in my heart is glad.
You have a kind voice that keeps company with the moonlight —
sing, until the earth's waiting turns wet.
It has gone travelling with no moonlight on it.
Sing —
it has opened its arms to come back with the moonlight.
Your image came home from the journey
and lay down to sleep on the shadows.
The morning light of your throat shone into my ear.
I cannot tell whether the smell of cloud is coming
or whether the grief in my heart is glad.
You have a kind voice
that is the moonlight's twin.
The sound is coming from the moon —
sing:
the song is free.
Tehran 1382

===lantern===

The morning light of your throat shone into my ear
and a green wakefulness stroked my hearing.
I cannot tell whether the smell of cloud is coming
or whether the grief in my heart is glad.
You have a kind voice that keeps company with the moonlight —
sing, until the earth's waiting turns wet.
It has gone travelling with no moonlight on it.
Sing.
It has opened its arms to come back with the moonlight.
Your image came home from the journey
and lay down to sleep on the shadows.
The morning light of your throat shone into my ear.
I cannot tell whether the smell of cloud is coming
or whether the grief in my heart is glad.
You have a kind voice
that is the moonlight's twin.
The sound is coming from the moon.
Sing:
the song is free.

Tehran 1382

